fix(scripts): scan docs/adr/, the one docs directory that ships

The operator-path gate skipped all of `docs/` with the comment "local-only,
never pushed". That was true of `docs/` when the line was written and stopped
being true when ADRs started shipping. `docs/adr/` is tracked and is in every
clone, so the one docs directory that reaches the public repository was the
one directory the gate could not see.

Measured before the fix. A file carrying an operator home path inside
`docs/adr/` produced "none names an operator path" - a false pass, in the gate
whose whole job is to prevent one.

`docs/adr/` is now scanned like any other shipped file and the rest of `docs/`
is still skipped, because that part genuinely is local-only.

The selftest gets case 7, which plants an operator path inside `docs/adr/` and
requires the gate to catch it, and case 6 is strengthened to hold the other
direction. Case 6 was vacuous, since `docs/*` is gitignored and its plain
`git add` added nothing at all, so the gate never saw the file and the case
passed on a green that said nothing about the skip it exists to test. It now
forces the plant into the index, which leaves the gate's own exclusion as the
only thing that can keep the file from being read. Both directions, or the
exclusion gets widened back by whoever finds it inconvenient.

The plant literals are still assembled from fragments, so the selftest does
not contain what it plants and needs no exemption from the gate.

The lesson is in the comment rather than in the code, and the header now says
so. A comment stating a fact goes stale silently; one stating the reason fails
out loud when the reason stops holding.

Driven by case 7 of scripts/no-operator-paths-selftest.sh, watched failing
first against the unchanged gate.

